  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nUpper troughing over the New England area will pull away as we head \ninto Monday, with WNW upper flow remaining overhead through midweek \nalong with weak high pressure at the surface. This will keep dry \nconditions in place, with somewhat chilly mornings but then \ncomfortable fall temperatures during the afternoon. \n\nHowever, the main focus of the entirety of the forecast period will \nbe the cold upper troughing forecast to affect the eastern CONUS \nduring the latter part of this week. A strong shortwave will drop \nsouth out of Canada Wed afternoon and evening, phasing with a \nstrengthening subtropical jet over the southern tier states Wed \nnight into Thu to drive a cold front through the forecast area \nsometime during the early morning hours on Thursday most likely. \nNothing stands out with this front in terms of severe weather or \nheavy rain potential, although in looking at forecast soundings I \nwouldn't be at all surprised to see some embedded thunder in the \nrainfall that occurs as mid level lapse rates steepen considerably \nduring that time. By Thursday night, a strong upper low forms over \nthe Ohio valley and eastern Great Lakes region. On the upstream side \nof this upper trough/closed low, there's potential for H85 \ntemperatures to drop significantly, potentially falling a few \ndegrees below 0C as NW flow persists over the area as we head into \nthe end of the week and first part of next weekend. Does this mean \nwe could see some snowfall in the higher terrain? Well, the overall \npattern is certainly conducive for it. However there are far too \nmany details yet to be sorted out to start worrying about it right \nnow. Suffice it to say that it's something we'll be keeping an eye \non, but the main takeaway for the latter part of the week is that \nconditions will likely turn starkly colder, with highs going from a \nfew degrees above normal on Wed, to near normal on Thu, to \npossibly +10 degrees below normal by Friday afternoon. The cool\nconditions will then continue into the weekend.",
